<!-- PART A : SETS                                                      -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<h2 class="part" id="partA">Part A — Sets, Venn Diagrams &amp; Surveys</h2>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A1.</span> Prove that \(A\cap(A\cup B)=A\cap B\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <div class="note"><strong>Printed question is false as written.</strong> By the absorption law, \(A\cap(A\cup B)=A\). The equality \(A\cap(A\cup B)=A\cap B\) holds only when \(A\subseteq B\). Counter‑example: \(A=\{1,2\},\;B=\{3\}\) gives \(A\cap(A\cup B)=\{1,2\}\) but \(A\cap B=\varnothing\).</div>
    <p><strong>Proof of the absorption law \(A\cap(A\cup B)=A\).</strong> Let \(x\) be arbitrary:</p>
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    x\in A\cap(A\cup B)
    &\iff x\in A \ \text{and}\ x\in A\cup B\\
    &\iff x\in A \ \text{and}\ (x\in A \ \text{or}\ x\in B)\\
    &\iff x\in A .
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    <p>Hence \(A\cap(A\cup B)\subseteq A\) and \(A\subseteq A\cap(A\cup B)\), so the sets are equal. \(\blacksquare\)</p>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(A\cap(A\cup B)=A\). The printed right‑hand side \(A\cap B\) is a typographical error.</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Special paper Q1(g) · Supplementary 2024 Q1(g)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A2.</span> Prove that \((A\cup B)\cap(A\cup C)=A\cup(B\cap C)\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let \(x\) be arbitrary.</p>
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    x\in (A\cup B)\cap(A\cup C)
    &\iff (x\in A \text{ or } x\in B)\ \text{and}\ (x\in A \text{ or } x\in C)\\
    &\iff x\in A \ \text{or}\ (x\in B \text{ and } x\in C)\\
    &\iff x\in A\cup(B\cap C).
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    <p>The middle step is the distributive law of “or” over “and” in logic. \(\blacksquare\)</p>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \((A\cup B)\cap(A\cup C)=A\cup(B\cap C)\) — distributivity of \(\cup\) over \(\cap\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (5) Q2(a) · Main paper (8) Q2(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A3.</span> Prove that \(\big((A\cap B)\cup C\big)=(A\cup C)\cap(B\cup C)\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <div class="note"><strong>Printed right side is \((A\cup C)\cup(B\cup C)\)</strong>, which is false (take \(A=\{1\},B=\varnothing,C=\varnothing\): LHS \(=\varnothing\), RHS \(=\{1\}\)). The correct connective is \(\cap\).</div>
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    x\in (A\cap B)\cup C
    &\iff (x\in A \text{ and } x\in B)\ \text{or}\ x\in C\\
    &\iff (x\in A \text{ or } x\in C)\ \text{and}\ (x\in B \text{ or } x\in C)\\
    &\iff x\in (A\cup C)\cap(B\cup C). \qquad\blacksquare
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \((A\cap B)\cup C=(A\cup C)\cap(B\cup C)\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Special paper Q3(c)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">5 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A4.</span> Prove that \((A\cap B)^{c}=A^{c}\cup B^{c}\) by the element‑argument method (De Morgan’s law).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let the universal set be \(U\) and let \(x\in U\) be arbitrary.</p>
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    x\in (A\cap B)^{c}
    &\iff \neg\big(x\in A\cap B\big)\\
    &\iff \neg\big(x\in A\ \text{and}\ x\in B\big)\\
    &\iff \neg(x\in A)\ \text{or}\ \neg(x\in B)\\
    &\iff x\in A^{c}\ \text{or}\ x\in B^{c}\\
    &\iff x\in A^{c}\cup B^{c}.
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    <p>Each step is a biconditional, so \((A\cap B)^{c}\subseteq A^{c}\cup B^{c}\) and \(A^{c}\cup B^{c}\subseteq (A\cap B)^{c}\); hence equality. \(\blacksquare\)</p>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \((A\cap B)^{c}=A^{c}\cup B^{c}\) — De Morgan’s first law.</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Part‑time paper Q1(c)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A5.</span> Prove that \(\overline{\big((A\cap B)\cup C\big)}=\overline{(A\cup C)}\cup\overline{(B\cup C)}\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Write \(\overline{X}=X^{c}\). Using De Morgan and distributivity:</p>
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    \overline{(A\cap B)\cup C}
    &=(A\cap B)^{c}\cap C^{c}\\
    &=(A^{c}\cup B^{c})\cap C^{c}\\
    &=(A^{c}\cap C^{c})\cup(B^{c}\cap C^{c})\\
    &=\overline{(A\cup C)}\cup\overline{(B\cup C)}. \qquad\blacksquare
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    <div class="note">The paper prints \(\cap\) between the two complement terms; as shown, the correct connective is \(\cup\).</div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(\overline{(A\cap B)\cup C}=\overline{(A\cup C)}\cup\overline{(B\cup C)}\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (7) Q2(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">3 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A6.</span> Prove that \(A\cup(B-A)=A\cup B\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
    x\in A\cup(B-A)
    &\iff x\in A \ \text{or}\ (x\in B \text{ and } x\notin A)\\
    &\iff x\in A\ \text{or}\ x\in B\\
    &\iff x\in A\cup B. \qquad\blacksquare
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(A\cup(B-A)=A\cup B\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (8) Q5(c)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A7.</span> Given \(A=\{a,b,c\}\), \(B=\{c,d,e\}\) and \(C=\{d,e,f\}\), show that \(A\cap(B\cup C)=(A\cap B)\cup(A\cap C)\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>\(B\cup C=\{c,d,e,f\}\), so</p>
    \[
    A\cap(B\cup C)=\{a,b,c\}\cap\{c,d,e,f\}=\{c\}.
    \]
    <p>Also \(A\cap B=\{c\}\) and \(A\cap C=\{a,b,c\}\cap\{d,e,f\}=\varnothing\), so</p>
    \[
    (A\cap B)\cup(A\cap C)=\{c\}\cup\varnothing=\{c\}.
    \]
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Both sides equal \(\{c\}\), so the expressions <em>are</em> equal. (The paper asks to show “\(\neq\)” — a misprint; the distributive law gives equality.)</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (8) Q1(h)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A8.</span> Let \(A=\{a,b,c,d\}\), \(B=\{a,c,e\}\), \(C=\{a,e\}\). Show that \(A\cap(B\cap C)=(A\cap B)\cap C\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>\(B\cap C=\{a,c,e\}\cap\{a,e\}=\{a\}\), hence</p>
    \[
    A\cap(B\cap C)=\{a,b,c,d\}\cap\{a\}=\{a\}.
    \]
    <p>Also \(A\cap B=\{a,c\}\), hence</p>
    \[
    (A\cap B)\cap C=\{a,c\}\cap\{a,e\}=\{a\}.
    \]
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Both sides \(=\{a\}\). Intersection is associative.</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Supplementary 2024 Q3(c)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">5 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A9.</span> Let \(A=\{0,1,2,3,4\}\), \(B=\{2,3,q,r,s\}\), \(C=\{2,r,p\}\). Find (i) \(A\cup B\) (ii) \(A\cup C\) (iii) \(A\cup B\cup C\) (iv) \(A\cap B\) (v) \(A\cap B\cap C\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>(i) \(A\cup B=\{0,1,2,3,4,q,r,s\}\)</li>
      <li>(ii) \(A\cup C=\{0,1,2,3,4,p,r\}\)</li>
      <li>(iii) \(A\cup B\cup C=\{0,1,2,3,4,p,q,r,s\}\)</li>
      <li>(iv) \(A\cap B=\{2,3\}\)</li>
      <li>(v) \(A\cap B\cap C=\{2\}\)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Part‑time paper Q1(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">2 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A10.</span> Find the cardinality of the power set of \(A=\{1,2,3,4\}\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>\(|A|=4\), so \(\big|\mathcal{P}(A)\big|=2^{4}=16\).</p>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(|\mathcal{P}(A)|=16\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Special paper Q2(e) · Supplementary 2024 Q2(e)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">3 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A11.</span> Given \(A=\{a,e,o,u\}\), find \(\mathcal{P}(A)\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>\(|A|=4\), so there are \(2^{4}=16\) subsets:</p>
    <p>\(\varnothing,\ \{a\},\ \{e\},\ \{o\},\ \{u\},\ \{a,e\},\ \{a,o\},\ \{a,u\},\ \{e,o\},\ \{e,u\},\ \{o,u\},\ \{a,e,o\},\ \{a,e,u\},\ \{a,o,u\},\ \{e,o,u\},\ \{a,e,o,u\}\).</p>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (5) Q4(d) · Main paper (7) Q4(d)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">3 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A12.</span> Given \(A=\{a,b,c\}\), find the power set of \(A\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>\(\mathcal{P}(A)=\big\{\varnothing,\{a\},\{b\},\{c\},\{a,b\},\{a,c\},\{b,c\},\{a,b,c\}\big\}\), so \(|\mathcal{P}(A)|=2^{3}=8\).</p>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (8) Q3(d)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">5 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A13.</span> Two sets have \(m\) and \(k\) elements. If the total number of subsets of the first set is 112 more than that of the second set, find the values of \(m\) and \(k\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    \[
    2^{m}-2^{k}=112 \;\Longrightarrow\; 2^{k}\big(2^{m-k}-1\big)=112 .
    \]
    <p>Since \(112=16\times 7=2^{4}(2^{3}-1)\), we match factors:</p>
    \[
    2^{k}=2^{4}\Rightarrow k=4,\qquad 2^{m-k}-1=7\Rightarrow m-k=3\Rightarrow m=7 .
    \]
    <p><em>Check:</em> \(2^{7}-2^{4}=128-16=112\). ✓</p>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(m=7,\ k=4\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (6) Q1(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">6 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A14.</span> State whether each statement is true or false:<br>
    (i) \(\{a,b,c\}=\{a,a,b,c\}\)<br>
    (ii) \(\{a,b,e\}=\{e,b,a,c\}\)<br>
    (iii) \(\{1,2,3\}=\{2,1,1\}\)<br>
    (iv) \(\{x:x^{2}=4\}=\{x:-2,-1,0,1,2\}\)<br>
    (v) \(\{x:|x|\le 2\}=\{x:-2\le x\le 2\}\)<br>
    (vi) The empty set \(\varnothing\) is a subset of any set and a set is always a subset of itself.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>(i) <strong>True</strong> — repetition does not change a set.</li>
      <li>(ii) <strong>False</strong> — \(c\notin\{a,b,e\}\).</li>
      <li>(iii) <strong>False</strong> — \(3\notin\{2,1,1\}=\{1,2\}\).</li>
      <li>(iv) <strong>False</strong> — \(\{x:x^{2}=4\}=\{-2,2\}\neq\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}\).</li>
      <li>(v) <strong>True</strong> — both describe the closed interval \([-2,2]\).</li>
      <li>(vi) <strong>True</strong> — \(\varnothing\subseteq X\) and \(X\subseteq X\) for every set \(X\).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (6) Q2(a)</span>
</div>

<h3 class="sub">A‑II. Venn‑diagram survey problems</h3>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">4 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A15.</span> In a survey of 400 students in a college, 100 were listed as studying typing (T) and 150 as doing accounting (A); 75 were registered for both courses. Find (i) the number not registered for either course, (ii) the number registered for typing only.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>\(|T\cup A|=100+150-75=175\).</p>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 250" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: typing and accounting">
      <title>Typing (T) and Accounting (A)</title>
      <circle cx="150" cy="125" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="250" cy="125" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <text class="name" x="90" y="48">T</text>
      <text class="name" x="300" y="48">A</text>
      <text x="105" y="130">25</text>
      <text x="189" y="130">75</text>
      <text x="278" y="130">75</text>
      <text x="352" y="60">225</text>
      <text x="340" y="80" class="small">neither</text>
    </svg>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>(i) Neither \(=400-175=\mathbf{225}\)</li>
      <li>(ii) Typing only \(=100-75=\mathbf{25}\)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> (i) 225 students, (ii) 25 students.</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Special paper Q4(c) · Supplementary 2024 Q4(c)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">7 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A16.</span> In a survey of 70 students, 28 know English, 29 know French and 26 know German. 11 know English and French, 12 know French and German, 7 know English and German, and 5 know all three languages. Find (i) the number who know at least one language, (ii) exactly two languages, (iii) English and French but not German, (iv) none of the languages.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let \(E,F,G\) denote the three languages, with triple intersection \(=5\).</p>
    \[
    |E\cup F\cup G|=28+29+26-11-12-7+5=58 .
    \]
    <p>Region counts:</p>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>\(E\) only \(=28-11-7+5=15\)</li>
      <li>\(F\) only \(=29-11-12+5=11\)</li>
      <li>\(G\) only \(=26-12-7+5=12\)</li>
      <li>\(E\cap F\) only \(=11-5=6\)</li>
      <li>\(F\cap G\) only \(=12-5=7\)</li>
      <li>\(E\cap G\) only \(=7-5=2\)</li>
    </ul>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 340" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: English, French, German">
      <title>English (E), French (F), German (G)</title>
      <circle cx="145" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="255" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <circle cx="200" cy="215" r="85" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.12)" stroke="#f59e0b"/>
      <text class="name" x="58" y="52">E</text>
      <text class="name" x="325" y="52">F</text>
      <text class="name" x="192" y="332">G</text>
      <text x="102" y="120">15</text>
      <text x="193" y="110">6</text>
      <text x="286" y="120">11</text>
      <text x="142" y="197">2</text>
      <text x="243" y="197">7</text>
      <text x="193" y="283">12</text>
      <text x="193" y="170">5</text>
      <text x="350" y="58">12</text>
      <text x="338" y="78" class="small">none</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="ans">
      (i) At least one \(=\mathbf{58}\)<br>
      (ii) Exactly two \(=6+7+2=\mathbf{15}\)<br>
      (iii) English &amp; French but not German \(=\mathbf{6}\)<br>
      (iv) None \(=70-58=\mathbf{12}\)
    </div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (6) Q1(b)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">9 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A17.</span> Each of 100 first‑year students studies at least one of Mathematics, Electronics and Accounting. 65 study Mathematics, 45 Electronics, 42 Accounting, 20 Mathematics and Electronics, 25 Mathematics and Accounting, 15 Electronics and Accounting. Find (i) the number studying all three, (ii) Mathematics and Electronics but not Accounting, (iii) only Electronics.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let \(x\) be the number studying all three. Since every student studies at least one subject:</p>
    \[
    100=65+45+42-20-25-15+x=92+x \;\Longrightarrow\; x=8 .
    \]
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>(ii) \(M\cap E\) only \(=20-8=12\)</li>
      <li>(iii) \(E\) only \(=45-20-15+8=18\)</li>
    </ul>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 340" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: Mathematics, Electronics, Accounting">
      <title>Mathematics (M), Electronics (E), Accounting (A)</title>
      <circle cx="145" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="255" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <circle cx="200" cy="215" r="85" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.12)" stroke="#f59e0b"/>
      <text class="name" x="58" y="52">M</text>
      <text class="name" x="325" y="52">E</text>
      <text class="name" x="192" y="332">A</text>
      <text x="102" y="120">28</text>
      <text x="193" y="110">12</text>
      <text x="286" y="120">18</text>
      <text x="142" y="197">17</text>
      <text x="243" y="197">7</text>
      <text x="193" y="283">10</text>
      <text x="193" y="170">8</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> (i) 8, (ii) 12, (iii) 18.</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (5) Q4(a) · Main paper (7) Q4(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">9 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A18.</span> Out of 120 students, 70 study Physics, 60 Chemistry, 50 Biology, 30 Physics and Chemistry, 25 Physics and Biology, 20 Chemistry and Biology. Find (i) all three, (ii) Physics and Chemistry but not Biology, (iii) only Chemistry.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let \(x\) = all three. Every student studies at least one subject:</p>
    \[
    120=70+60+50-30-25-20+x=105+x\;\Longrightarrow\;x=15 .
    \]
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>(ii) \(P\cap C\) only \(=30-15=15\)</li>
      <li>(iii) \(C\) only \(=60-30-20+15=25\)</li>
    </ul>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 340" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: Physics, Chemistry, Biology">
      <title>Physics (P), Chemistry (C), Biology (B)</title>
      <circle cx="145" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="255" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <circle cx="200" cy="215" r="85" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.12)" stroke="#f59e0b"/>
      <text class="name" x="58" y="52">P</text>
      <text class="name" x="325" y="52">C</text>
      <text class="name" x="192" y="332">B</text>
      <text x="102" y="120">30</text>
      <text x="193" y="110">15</text>
      <text x="286" y="120">25</text>
      <text x="142" y="197">10</text>
      <text x="243" y="197">5</text>
      <text x="193" y="283">20</text>
      <text x="193" y="170">15</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> (i) 15, (ii) 15, (iii) 25.</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (8) Q3(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">10 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A19.</span> Out of 1191 middle‑school students: 807 use Facebook, 707 use YouTube, 908 use iTunes; 526 use Facebook and YouTube, 667 use YouTube and iTunes, 593 use Facebook and iTunes; 489 use all three. Find (i) iTunes only, (ii) exactly two websites, (iii) exactly one website, (iv) none, (v) at least two.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Region counts (subtract the triple from each pairwise figure):</p>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>Facebook only \(=807-526-593+489=177\)</li>
      <li>YouTube only \(=707-526-667+489=3\)</li>
      <li>iTunes only \(=908-667-593+489=137\)</li>
      <li>FB &amp; YT only \(=526-489=37\)</li>
      <li>YT &amp; iT only \(=667-489=178\)</li>
      <li>FB &amp; iT only \(=593-489=104\)</li>
      <li>All three \(=489\)</li>
    </ul>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 340" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: Facebook, YouTube, iTunes">
      <title>Facebook (F), YouTube (Y), iTunes (I)</title>
      <circle cx="145" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="255" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <circle cx="200" cy="215" r="85" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.12)" stroke="#f59e0b"/>
      <text class="name" x="58" y="52">F</text>
      <text class="name" x="325" y="52">Y</text>
      <text class="name" x="192" y="332">I</text>
      <text x="98" y="120">177</text>
      <text x="193" y="110">37</text>
      <text x="288" y="120">3</text>
      <text x="138" y="197">104</text>
      <text x="243" y="197">178</text>
      <text x="193" y="283">137</text>
      <text x="193" y="170">489</text>
      <text x="348" y="58">66</text>
      <text x="336" y="78" class="small">none</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="ans">
      (i) iTunes only \(=\mathbf{137}\)<br>
      (ii) Exactly two \(=37+178+104=\mathbf{319}\)<br>
      (iii) Exactly one \(=177+3+137=\mathbf{317}\)<br>
      (iv) None \(=1191-(177+3+137+37+178+104+489)=1191-1125=\mathbf{66}\)<br>
      (v) At least two \(=37+178+104+489=\mathbf{808}\)
    </div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (6) Q5(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">5 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A20.</span> In a class of 50 Kabarak University students, 30 are studying calculus, 25 are studying complex analysis and 10 are studying both units. (i) Represent the information in a Venn diagram (calculus = A, complex analysis = B). (ii) How many students are studying either unit? (iii) Determine \(\left|A^{c}\cap B^{c}\right|\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Calculus only \(=30-10=20\); complex analysis only \(=25-10=15\).</p>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 250" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: calculus and complex analysis">
      <title>Calculus (A) and Complex Analysis (B)</title>
      <circle cx="150" cy="125" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="250" cy="125" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <text class="name" x="90" y="48">A</text>
      <text class="name" x="300" y="48">B</text>
      <text x="105" y="130">20</text>
      <text x="189" y="130">10</text>
      <text x="278" y="130">15</text>
      <text x="352" y="60">5</text>
      <text x="340" y="80" class="small">neither</text>
    </svg>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>(ii) \(|A\cup B|=30+25-10=\mathbf{45}\)</li>
      <li>(iii) \(|A^{c}\cap B^{c}|=|(A\cup B)^{c}|=50-45=\mathbf{5}\)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Part‑time paper Q1(b)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">10 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A21.</span> A market researcher investigating consumers’ preference for three brands of beverages — coffee, tea and cocoa — in Kabarak University gathered the following information. From a sample of 800 consumers, 230 took coffee, 245 took tea and 325 took cocoa, 30 took all the beverages, 70 took coffee and cocoa, 110 took coffee only and 185 took cocoa only. Required: (i) a Venn diagram; (ii) the number who took tea only; (iii) tea and coffee only; (iv) tea and cocoa only.</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let \(C\) = coffee, \(T\) = tea, \(K\) = cocoa.</p>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li>Coffee &amp; cocoa only \(=70-30=40\)</li>
      <li>Coffee &amp; tea only: \(110+x+40+30=230\Rightarrow x=50\)</li>
      <li>Cocoa &amp; tea only: \(185+40+y+30=325\Rightarrow y=70\)</li>
      <li>Tea only: \(245-\big(50+70+30\big)=95\)</li>
    </ul>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 400 340" role="img" aria-label="Venn diagram: coffee, tea, cocoa">
      <title>Coffee (C), Tea (T), Cocoa (K)</title>
      <circle cx="145" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.12)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="255" cy="130" r="85" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="#10b981"/>
      <circle cx="200" cy="215" r="85" fill="rgba(245,158,11,.12)" stroke="#f59e0b"/>
      <text class="name" x="58" y="52">C</text>
      <text class="name" x="325" y="52">T</text>
      <text class="name" x="192" y="332">K</text>
      <text x="98" y="120">110</text>
      <text x="193" y="110">50</text>
      <text x="288" y="120">95</text>
      <text x="138" y="197">40</text>
      <text x="243" y="197">70</text>
      <text x="193" y="283">185</text>
      <text x="193" y="170">30</text>
      <text x="350" y="58">220</text>
      <text x="336" y="78" class="small">none</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> (ii) tea only \(=95\); (iii) tea &amp; coffee only \(=50\); (iv) tea &amp; cocoa only \(=70\). (None \(=220\).)</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Part‑time paper Q4(a)</span>
</div>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">5 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A22.</span> If \(A\) and \(B\) are two sets such that \(n(B-A)=2\,n(A-B)=4\,n(A\cap B)\) and \(n(A\cup B)=14\), find \(n\big(\mathcal{P}(A)\big)\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p>Let \(n(A\cap B)=x\). Then \(n(A-B)=2x\) and \(n(B-A)=4x\).</p>
    \[
    n(A\cup B)=4x+2x+x=7x=14\;\Longrightarrow\;x=2 .
    \]
    \[
    n(A)=n(A-B)+n(A\cap B)=2x+x=3x=6 .
    \]
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(n(A)=6\), so \(\big|\mathcal{P}(A)\big|=2^{6}=\mathbf{64}\).</div>
  </div>
  <span class="src">Main paper (6) Q4(a)</span>
</div>

<h3 class="sub">A‑III. Venn‑diagram illustrations</h3>

<div class="q">
  <span class="marks">8 marks</span>
  <div class="question"><span class="qnum">A23.</span> Use Venn diagrams to illustrate the following sets: (i) \(A\cap B\) (ii) \(A\cup B\) (iii) \(A\triangle B\) (symmetric difference) (iv) \(A\cup B\cup C\) (v) \(A-B\).</div>
  <div class="sol">
    <p><strong>(i) \(A\cap B\)</strong> — the overlap region.</p>
    <svg class="venn" viewBox="0 0 360 200" role="img" aria-label="A intersection B">
      <circle cx="130" cy="100" r="70" fill="rgba(37,99,235,.08)" stroke="#2563eb"/>
      <circle cx="230" cy="100" r="70" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.08)" stroke="#10b981"/>
